= Belief revision — Technical =

== AGM setting ==
Let K be a logically closed belief set.

Three core operations are:

<pre>
expansion:   K + φ
contraction: K ÷ φ
revision:    K * φ
</pre>

== Revision ==
Revision incorporates φ while preserving consistency when possible.

The Levi identity defines revision via contraction and expansion:

<pre>
K * φ = (K ÷ ¬φ) + φ
</pre>

for standard AGM constructions.

== Rationality postulates ==
AGM revision postulates include closure, success, inclusion, preservation, consistency, and extensionality, with supplementary conditions for conjunction.

Representation theorems connect these postulates to constructions such as partial-meet revision.

== Belief bases ==
A belief base is not closed under logical consequence.

Base revision can avoid some idealizations of AGM belief sets and changes the status of postulates such as recovery.

== Iterated revision ==
Repeated updates require constraints on how one revision changes the priorities governing later revisions.

This leads to ranking, plausibility, and epistemic-entrenchment models.

== Non-prioritized change ==
Traditional AGM revision gives new input high priority.

Non-prioritized systems allow rejection or partial acceptance of input when it conflicts with stronger commitments.

